{#- Beginner walkthrough deck: a four-card carousel teaching a first-time
    reader when to buy, the buy/sell lines, the review period, and the
    stop-loss -- one idea per card, in that order. Content derives from the
    tab-guide prose (index.html.j2's guide_body_leaderboard), restructured
    for sequential beginner pacing rather than copied verbatim. See
    sector_momentum-notes/specs/2026-09-09-beginner-walkthrough-deck-design.md.

    English only, deliberately -- no data-i18n/data-i18n-html anywhere in
    this file. Matches _methodology.html.j2's existing carve-out, which is
    what keeps tests/test_i18n_coverage.py from requiring a Swedish
    translation for content that doesn't have one yet.

    NOT inside {% if auth %} -- static public content, shown to guests and
    signed-in readers alike, exactly like the methodology modal beside it.

    Numbers here are LIVE, not typed out: default_horizon_top_n and
    trailing_stop_pct come from dashboard/build.py (computed once from
    src.horizons.default_horizon()/trailing_stop_frac(), same pattern as
    the existing exit_rank_today value) -- see Global Constraints in the
    implementation plan for why. Card 3's cadence word ("monthly") is the
    one exception: a natural-language word doesn't interpolate cleanly, so
    it's protected by a factual-anchor test instead
    (tests/test_beginner_deck.py::test_deck_cadence_matches_the_live_default_horizon),
    mirroring tests/test_methodology.py's own pattern for the same problem. -#}
<div class="methodology-overlay" id="beginner-deck-modal" hidden>
  <div class="methodology-modal beginner-deck" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="beginner-deck-title">
    <button type="button" class="methodology-close" id="beginner-deck-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    <h2 id="beginner-deck-title">How this works</h2>

    <div class="beginner-deck-card" data-step="1">
      <h3>When to buy in</h3>
      <p>You buy from the top of the list. Themes are ranked strongest to
         weakest today. The buy band is the top {{ default_horizon_top_n }}
         &mdash; those are the only themes worth buying fresh right now.
         Rank 1 only means &ldquo;best of the group today,&rdquo; not
         &ldquo;guaranteed to go up.&rdquo;</p>
    </div>

    <div class="beginner-deck-card" data-step="2" hidden>
      <h3>The buy/sell lines</h3>
      <p>Two lines, not one. There&rsquo;s a line for buying and a
         separate, looser line for selling &mdash; with quiet room between
         them, on purpose. A theme drifting a rank or two doesn&rsquo;t get
         sold; only one that clearly leaves the wider band does.
         <strong>This is not a stop-loss</strong> &mdash; it only looks at
         rank against other themes, never at price, and it only checks at
         the next scheduled review, never in between.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="beginner-deck-card" data-step="3" hidden>
      <h3>The review period</h3>
      <p>You look on a schedule, not constantly. The dashboard tells you
         when to look next &mdash; monthly for the default horizon.
         Reviewing more often doesn&rsquo;t mean better decisions, it means
         more trading, and every trade has a real cost. Between reviews,
         just wait.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="beginner-deck-card" data-step="4" hidden>
      <h3>The stop-loss</h3>
      <p>A safety net, separate from the lines above. If a theme
         you&rsquo;ve starred closes {{ trailing_stop_pct }}% below its
         highest point since you starred it, you get a one-time alert and
         a dashboard marker. It measures from the peak since you starred
         it &mdash; not what you paid &mdash; and it never sells anything
         automatically. It&rsquo;s the one thing here that reacts to price
         between reviews, not just rank.</p>
      <p><a href="#methodology" id="beginner-deck-methodology-link">Want more detail? Read the full Methodology.</a></p>
    </div>

    <div id="beginner-deck-dots" class="beginner-deck-dots" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="beginner-deck-nav">
      <button type="button" id="beginner-deck-back">Back</button>
      <button type="button" id="beginner-deck-next">Next</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
(function () {
  var overlay = document.getElementById("beginner-deck-modal");
  if (!overlay) { return; }
  var modal = window.SMModal.bind(overlay, {
    closeBtn: document.getElementById("beginner-deck-close"),
    // Every open() call -- footer link, methodology cross-link, and
    // maybeAutoShow() -- resets to Card 1 in this one place, so a returning
    // reader who dismissed the deck on Card 4 last time never sees a stray
    // "Back" button or missing progress dots on reopen. Runs before
    // overlay.hidden flips to false (see _modal.js.j2's open()), so there is
    // no flash of the wrong card first.
    onOpen: function () { if (window.BeginnerDeck) { window.BeginnerDeck.goToStep(1); } },
  });
  if (!modal) { return; }
  window.SMBeginnerDeckModal = modal;

  var link = document.getElementById("beginner-deck-link");
  if (link) {
    link.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      modal.open();
    });
  }

  // Card 4's "Read the full Methodology" link -- mirrors the reverse
  // direction's handler in _methodology.html.j2 (methodology-to-deck-link).
  var methodologyLink = document.getElementById("beginner-deck-methodology-link");
  if (methodologyLink) {
    methodologyLink.addEventListener("click", function (e) {
      e.preventDefault();
      modal.close();
      if (window.SMMethodologyModal) {
        window.SMMethodologyModal.open();
      }
    });
  }
})();
</script>
<script src="{{ asset_url('beginner-deck.js') }}"></script>
